        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Check the magnet, the gap and the wiring before buying
            anything.</strong> Most speed sensor faults are a magnet that has moved, a bracket
            that has been knocked or a connector that is half out, and all three are found with the
            hood off and the machine unplugged.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Test, then substitute, then suspect the controller.</strong> A
            reed sensor is a two-minute multimeter check, a new sensor is cheap enough to be a
            diagnostic step, and only when both are good is the board the likely fault — at which
            point the
            <a href="/treadmill-repair-guide/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">repair-or-replace arithmetic</a>
            matters. And a belt that surges on its own is a stop-now fault, not one to live with.
          </p>
        </div>
